\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ul\b\f0\fs40\lang9 Offense vs. Defense (OvD): A 10,000-Foot View\par
\ulnone\b0\i\fs24 Thesis: OvD looks like a fight over flags, but it is really a siege over a single binary - whether a fortified room's teleport-disruption coverage holds - and every weapon, item, and movement is a lever on that one contest of attrition, information, and tempo.\par
\i0 On its surface, OvD is territorial capture-the-flag: two standing teams, one attacking and one defending, where the offense wins only by owning every flag simultaneously long enough to run out a victory timer. But flags are not carried trophies; they are control points inside turret-defended base rooms reached through a funnel of portal-and-door chokepoints. The map's geometry is therefore the real board. A base is a graded depth - from the open main map, inward through successively tighter chokes, to the turret room and the flag at its heart - and both teams read that depth and arrange themselves along it: attackers pressing forward, defenders layered with disposable bodies forward and irreplaceable anchors deep.\par
The decisive insight - the one that reorganizes everything else - is \b "TD and out."\b0  An attacker who reaches and flips a flag is useless if he cannot leave; the flag room is sealed by teleport-disruption coverage, projected by two sources, the Field Medic and the sentry turret. While either lives, the attacker is trapped, dies, and his flip reverts. So the entire war collapses to one objective function: the offense must \b destroy the teleport-disruption sources before committing to the flag,\b0  and the defense must \b keep at least one alive at any cost.\b0  This is why turrets matter beyond their guns and why the medic is the spine of the defense - both are, first and foremost, locks on the door.\par
Everything beneath that pivot is the \b attrition economy\b0  that decides who wins the lock. Damage is gated by energy: a full-suit target shrugs off most fire, so combat is a two-step of drain, then finish - shotguns, CAWs, and beamers strip shields while teammates clean up. Survival is gated by resources: the defense sustains itself cheaply - the engineer mines ore to repair turrets and feeds crystals to the medic, who heals and drains in turn - while the offense, having no medic of its own, sustains through the Squad Leader's summon, reinforcing its dead back to the front, and through shared consumables like a stim pack handed to a wounded leader. Killing a turret rather than merely chipping it is leverage, because a rebuild costs many times more than a repair; the base bleeds out when its turrets fall.\par
Layered atop attrition is \b coordination,\b0  the discipline that separates a comp from a crowd. Defensive repulsor fields are spent in a deliberate 'rep order,' roles taking turns shielding the turrets so coverage never lapses; offensive repulsor charges are hoarded and staggered to punch through the wall of fire at the final chokes. The base's layout even dictates the roster - chokier rooms want a Heavy raining splash on the inner chokes, while open ones trade it for another body forward.\par
And wrapping all of it is \b information and tempo.\b0  The Medic and Squad Leader fight a private, bidirectional teleport-disruption duel, each denying the other's summon and escape while protecting his own. The Infiltrator turns the game into a sensor war, forcing defenders to burn energy regen on enhanced sensors or be caught off guard - his whole value resting on the imperfection of human timing. Even comp choice is a tempo decision: grind the base down with a heavy siege, or trade firepower for speed and disruption - droppacks, bouncing grenades, knockback charges - to reach the flag room at all costs before the defense can set.\par
So OvD is best understood not as a brawl over objectives but as a \b siege of a single lock.\b0  Geometry sets the board; the teleport-disruption pivot sets the goal; and attrition, resource logistics, coordinated cover, and information warfare are the four instruments by which one side preserves that lock for ninety seconds of dominance and the other prises it open. \b Win the contest over teleport-disruption coverage, and the flags follow.\b0\par
