\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ul\b\f0\fs36\lang9 Defense\par
\ulnone\b0\i\fs24 Defense commonly has 1 of 2 standard team compositions, but I'll just describe this layout: (1 Field Medic, 1 Combat Engineer, 1 Heavy Weapons, 2 Infantry). The infantries run different builds slightly different purposes. Going to discuss the tactics of each role on defense and how weapons and items are used, both offensively and defensively.\par
\b0\i\fs24 The second standard composition swaps the Heavy Weapons for a 3rd Infantry (also non-CAW, since only one CAW infantry is ever needed): 1 Field Medic, 1 Combat Engineer, 3 Infantry. It is typically run in bases like D7 (or N8, a duplicated D7 formation) - the base's layout drives the choice of composition.\par
'Rep Order' is an important concept for me to highlight as well. Every class gets a defensive item called "Repulsor Field" which consumes repulsor coils, 3 carried on their person, these deflect projectiles in an area around used, which protects the turrets and other teammates. The rep order is important because not everyone needs to use it all at once, often times roles will take turns. The order is\line\line Non-Caw Inf > Caw Inf > Field Medic > Combat Engineer > Heavy Weapons (with the last 3 being interchangeable depending on circumstances). What this practically means is a role will wait for the other role to either be out of repulsors or if they're not in position to rep for turrets.\ul\b\i0\fs22\par
\b0 Infantry\par
\ulnone\i Infantry are always more forward fighting then other support classes, draining, finishing, and drawing fire/distracting attackers so support classes can soften the attackers before they get to the turret room. They are also often times the first to use their 3 repulsor coils (repulsor field item) to defend turrets or their teammates.\ul\i0\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone ?b dinfcaw - This is the primary energy draining role on defense, using Kuchler A6 CAW as it is a spammable weapon that can be shot at mid and close range to soften shields for other teammates to finish attackers. This class is firing caw 80% of the time. \ul\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}\ulnone ?b dinf - This is the standard 'other infantry' build, more designed as 'the guy in the paint', typically the most vulnerable and in danger, the non-caw defensive infantry looks for finishing kills on drained targets, using defensive maneuvers (circle strafe, bouncing/gliding off of walls when out numbered overwhelmingly, using 'Repulsor Field' defensively to deflect incoming projectiles that would've landed or projectiles attempting to hurt turrets). Some players get really close and try to fire off two 'hail mary' rifle grenades often times before dying, others are more nuanced in their movements and looking for shotgun / assault rifle damage to finish off drained targets. This role is the most flexible in terms of their play style.\ul\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Heavy Weapons\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone ?b dhvy - Heavy Weapons typically stand in the back, using a combination of Makloc AC MK2 and Micro Missile Launcher to rain down supportive fire. When these weapons hit, they have splash damage, so often times hitting clustered groups is ideal. Protecting corners tightly with AC is effective, HW typically stand in the 'last half' of the flag's computed total path (essentially the last 2-3 choke points of the base before and amongst the turrets positioning). They often times have to rely on ammo drops for their limited ammo items (Micro Missile launcher consumes 2 rockets per firing, and 40 rockets are maximum, and often times builds dont even carry the full 40 rockets, so frequent replenishment is often necessary when the heavy is close to empty (or sometimes waits until empty to replenish). \ul\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Field Medic\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone Medic's typically are the primary anchor of defense, healing, draining attackers suit energy with Medic Beamer, staying in range to 'teleport disrupt' Squad Leader (usually from safety behind a wall unless defender's alive count is higher).\ul\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li1080\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone Medic Beamer - weapon that can wall bounce 3x, can be fired around corners to strategically bounce/create a wall of drain essentially as the attackers press forward.\ul\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}\ulnone Needler - Unused, only carried as a required item for Tranq\ul\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}\ulnone Tranq - The best crowd control ability in CTF, highly spammable, fast moving, and long stun duration. Often times tranq is used on a "pushout", or sometimes defensively while backpedaling as offense initially enters a base. It's also used to stun high velocity vehicles that approach the flag room so that the turrets can finish them!\ul\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}\ulnone Medikit - Area of effect heal center point the medic, only costs 1 Tsolvy Crystals, often used when at least 2 members have lost 20 health or more, or when 1 member is 75% or lower health.\ul\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}\ulnone Deluxe Medikit - Same as Medikit, except heals full and fast, costly at 5 Tsolvy Crystals per heal, used more seldom probably requires more dire circumstances like more teammates are critically low health for example.\ul\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}\ulnone Teleport Beacon - anchors a spot for 60 seconds in which the medic can teleport back to that spot within that 60 second window. This can add pressure to the defense when there is a backup anchor up (Heavy weapons or combat engineer) and if the medic needs a refresh and/or sees an opportune time to teleport disrupt the squad leader + deny the SL summon ability because of the TD ability. Using this often times follows up with a tranq to stun the SL, a draining weapon (Unittech smg i htink its in the standard medic build) to burst down the SL's energy, followed by a (close to) point blank frag grenade to finish them off (or another tranq to let other defenders finish them off).\ul\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Combat Engineer\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone This role is probably the most complicated to describe, as they are a combination of fighting, mining for resources on the map, repairing turrets, defensively repulsing for turrets, draining for their teammates with the Engy Beamer item they have, using 'Haywire grenades' to jam clusters of attackers, using teleport beacon + point blank haywire grenades as surprise attacks, placing traps like Grapeshot Mines, EMP Mines, etc. The most important thing is mining and repairing turrets, the repair item costs 5 titanium oxide and heals 75 hp so often times engineers will wait until 75 damage has been dealt (or 80% of it is close enough if all attackers have been cleared and wanting to 'top off' the turret health before going back to resuming mining on the map). There are spots on the map that contain these Titanium Oxide and Tsolvy Crystal materials which the engineer seeks out to pickup. They will drop Tsolvy Crystals for their medics once they come back to base. \par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\ul\b\fs36 Offense\par
\ulnone\b0\i\fs24 Offense coordinates their repulsor coils as well, to 'break through' the onslaught of defensive projectiles as they advance and take ground closer to the turret area, eventually breaking the base to capture the flags. Offensive runs typically focus on either maximizing turret damage or FINISHING a turret - a destroyed turret costs 25-50 Titanium Oxide to rebuild versus the much cheaper repair (5 oxide / 75 hp), so a kill is far more costly to the defense than mere damage.\par
\b0\i Compositions: the standard SL / HW / Infantry x3, or a more mobile, high-variance, high-speed SL / Jump Trooper / Infiltrator / Infantry x2 (trading the Heavy and 3rd Infantry for a Jump Trooper + Infiltrator).\par
\b 'TD and Out' - the core breakthrough concept: \b0 an attacker can only capture a flag and teleport OUT of the flag room if the teleport-disruption sources are down. Both the Field Medic and the sentry computer turret project teleport-disruption coverage over the room, so while EITHER is alive the attacker is trapped and cannot teleport out (and gets killed). Breaking a base therefore means killing the TD sources - the medic and the sentry turret - first, then capturing and teleporting out. So turrets are not just damage and the medic is not just heals; both are teleport-disruption coverage.\par
\b\i0\fs22 Infantry\par
\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0\i Chuck Frag and WP grenades at the turrets, continually diving back behind physics cover while not firing; situationally wait for a defender to poke around a corner, then opportunistically fire a rifle grenade.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Use the SHOTGUN to DRAIN targets first - undrained, full-suit-energy targets shrug off most damage until their shields are stripped.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b\i0\fs22 Heavy Weapons\par
\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0\i RPG is basically spammed as ground is gained - high velocity, high proximity-radius damage. It also has vehicle KNOCKBACK, usable creatively: hitting the SIDE of a hitbox displaces the target opposite the explosion, so a retreating defender can catch the lip of a wall and become easier to finish.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Once attackers reach a choke point where they can fire on the turrets, the team switches to PROTECTING the Heavy, who streams Maklov AC MK2 into the turrets in a steady stream; teammates with repulsor coils left spend them to shield the Heavy.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b\i0\fs22 Squad Leader\par
\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0\i Stays in the back, bouncing EB off walls or hitting targets directly from a distance. The CR weapon is used only as a finisher (seldom).\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Lays Grapeshot Mines or AP Mines in between Summon and EB - about 90% of what they should be doing is Summon + EB; an occasional mine when targets are far away, or when positioning defensively to respect the Field Medic's ability to warp on top of them (if the medic's Teleport Beacon is still up, be ready for it).\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Try to stay OUT of the medic's teleport-disruption range most of the time.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Also carries a Teleport Disruptor of their own, which disrupts the enemy Medic's Teleport Beacon and the Medic's summon item - the offensive mirror of the Medic teleport-disrupting the SL.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b\i0\fs22 Jump Trooper\par
\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0\i Occupies a DROPPACK (a vehicle they must CREATE first). On respawn they start the build's prefire timer; by the time the SL summons them the unoccupied droppack has spawned on the ground, and they occupy it immediately (with a small human-reaction delay before occupying their own freshly-created vehicle).\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Main weapon while on the droppack is the Maklov GL 8a - a 4-5 bounce grenade with inherited velocity, and droppacks have a very high top speed, so it is insanely disruptive: severe knockback plus a 'blender' effect. REPULSING it accelerates and extends its range significantly, so smart defenders are careful about repulsing this high-bounce item.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b\i0\fs22 Infiltrator\par
\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0\i Uses a beamer to DRAIN. Their Repulsor Field (Enhanced RFG, innate) knocks back enemy VEHICLES as well as projectiles (the standard repulsor knocks back only projectiles) - used to gain ground, or saved for a 'rush past the turrets to the flag room' run.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Advanced, hard-to-fully-describe play: run PAST enemies then repulse to knock defenders into the attackers from behind (via a pre-set Teleport Beacon, or simply running past).\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Forces defenders to carry Enhanced Sensors (ES) for detection range; ES costs energy regen, so defenders only run it when they suspect an infil is near - imperfect humans missing the right ES timing decide many games (surprise attacks).\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}When an infil sneaks past, an infantry sometimes waits briefly in the dropship to ATTACH and kill the nearest target in a surprise maneuver (not every run - roughly once every 5-8 runs).\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Also carries a disruptor item that stuns targets near walls - it only contacts walls and turrets (a vehicle-weight filter decides whether the projectile contacts).\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ul\b\i0\fs36 Universal Items (carried by every class)\par
\ulnone\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b0\i Energizer - grants 200 energy; every class carries 2.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Stim Pack - grants 10 health to oneself; every class can carry it. Freshly-respawning offensive attackers will often DROP a Stim Pack for their Squad Leader when the SL is low on HP from chip damage and has not had time to refresh - since offense has no medic of its own.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Repulsor Field - the defensive projectile-deflection item, consuming 3 repulsor coils (see Rep Order under Defense); coordinated on both sides.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Repulsor Charge - a THROWN repulsor coil that knocks back vehicles and players (relative to the explosion center) and drains energy; only 1 per class per respawn. Like a disjointed Enhanced RFG. Its use is staggered for coordination like the field coils, and a team saves about 2-3 of them for the turret approach - critical for gaining ground around chokes, especially the JT/Infiltrator 'reach the flag room at all costs' comp.\par
}
